          "paraphrase": "Seeing the citizens’ distress, the one Lord instantly becomes countless, compared to space in pots and reflected Suns. In each home he plays on stairs/swings/beds/terraces, laughs/eats with children, studies and teaches, and appears accompanied by the Kāśī king. Each householder rejoices. Oil massage, baths, footwashing, sixteen offerings, food, musk/sandal/flowers, clothing, service by wives/children/servants, sleep, Vedic recitation, song, sacred stories, Hari’s accounts, dance and lamps fill the visits. Some forms play with a noose. Purāṇa, dharmaśāstra and smṛti reading and each person’s wishes are fulfilled; Dīnanātha’s name is shown meaningful, with divine drums and flowers. The actual king cannot understand simultaneous royal/deity visits and dismisses the report before going to inspect.",
